# GitHub Issues for Research

The researcher uses a 5-level hierarchical issue structure to organize research:

1. **Encyclopedia Root** - Single root issue linking to all domains
2. **Domain** - Top-level domains (e.g., Science, Arts, History)
3. **Category** - Areas within domains (e.g., Science > Physics)
4. **Topic** - Specific subjects with article lists (e.g., Science > Physics > Quantum Mechanics)
5. **Article** - Individual encyclopedia entries (checkboxes in topic issues)

All levels are bidirectionally linked for easy navigation.

## Issue Types

Issues are classified using GitHub **Issue Types** (not labels):

| Level | Issue Type | Color |
|-------|------------|-------|
| Root | Encyclopedia Index | Gray |
| Domain | Research Domain | Orange |
| Category | Research Category | Blue |
| Topic | Research Topic | Purple |

The researcher monitors issues with **Research Topic** type and picks unchecked articles.

### Setting Issue Types via CLI

```bash
# Get issue node ID
gh api graphql -f query='query { repository(owner: "gitopedia", name: "gitopedia") { issue(number: 123) { id } } }'

# Apply issue type (use type ID from organization settings)
gh api graphql -f query='mutation { updateIssue(input: {id: "ISSUE_NODE_ID", issueTypeId: "TYPE_ID"}) { issue { issueType { name } } } }'
```

### Filtering Issues by Type (GraphQL)

Since issue types require GraphQL (not REST API labels), use queries like:

```bash
# Get all Research Topic issues
gh api graphql -f query='query {
  repository(owner: "gitopedia", name: "gitopedia") {
    issues(first: 100, states: OPEN, filterBy: {issueType: "Research Topic"}) {
      nodes { number title body }
    }
  }
}'
```

## Hierarchy Example

```
Encyclopedia Root (#126) [Encyclopedia Index]
└── Science (#127) [Research Domain]
    ├── Science > Physics (#124) [Research Category]
    │   ├── Science > Physics > Quantum Mechanics (#121) [Research Topic]
    │   │   └── Articles: [ ] Quantum Mechanics, [ ] Wave Function, ...
    │   └── Science > Physics > Thermodynamics (#122) [Research Topic]
    │       └── Articles: [ ] Entropy, [ ] Heat Transfer, ...
    └── Science > Geography (#125) [Research Category]
        └── Science > Geography > Plate Tectonics (#123) [Research Topic]
            └── Articles: [ ] Plate Tectonics, [ ] Continental Drift, ...
```

## Create Encyclopedia Root Issue

There should be only one root issue with Encyclopedia Index type:

```bash
gh issue create --repo gitopedia/gitopedia \
  --title "Encyclopedia Root" \
  --body "# Encyclopedia Root

Master index of all research domains in Gitopedia.

## Domains

- [Science](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/XXX)
- [Arts](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/XXX)"
```

Then set the issue type via GraphQL.

## Create a Domain Issue

```bash
gh issue create --repo gitopedia/gitopedia \
  --title "Science" \
  --body "# Science

Top-level domain for scientific research.

**Parent:** [Encyclopedia Root](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/126)

## Categories

- [Science > Physics](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/XXX)
- [Science > Geography](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/XXX)"
```

Then set the issue type to "Research Domain" via GraphQL.

## Create a Category Issue

```bash
gh issue create --repo gitopedia/gitopedia \
  --title "Science > Physics" \
  --body "# Science > Physics

Physics research topics under the Science domain.

**Domain:** [Science](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/127)

## Topics

- [Science > Physics > Quantum Mechanics](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/XXX)"
```

Then set the issue type to "Research Category" via GraphQL.

## Create a Topic Issue

Topic titles should include the full path: `Domain > Category > Topic`

```bash
gh issue create --repo gitopedia/gitopedia \
  --title "Science > Physics > Quantum Mechanics" \
  --body "# Quantum Mechanics

Articles about quantum mechanics to research.

**Category:** [Science > Physics](https://github.com/gitopedia/gitopedia/issues/124)

## Articles

- [ ] Quantum Mechanics
- [ ] Schrödinger's Cat
- [ ] Wave Function"
```

Then set the issue type to "Research Topic" via GraphQL.

## Issue Lifecycle

1. **Encyclopedia Root** - Single root issue created once
2. **Domain Created** - Links to root, root updated to link back
3. **Category Created** - Links to domain, domain updated to link back
4. **Topic Created** - Links to category, category updated to link back
5. **Topic Claimed** - Researcher assigns itself to prevent race conditions
6. **Articles Processed** - Researcher iterates through unchecked articles
7. **Summary Posted** - Researcher posts comment summarizing changes
8. **Topic Released** - Researcher unassigns itself when done

## How the Researcher Claims Topics

1. Fetches all open issues with "Research Topic" issue type via GraphQL
2. Filters to topics that are:
   - Not assigned to anyone
   - Don't have the `pending review` label
   - Don't have an open PR referencing them
3. Randomly selects one available topic
4. Claims the topic by assigning itself to the issue
5. Waits briefly to verify sole ownership (prevents race conditions)

## How the Researcher Processes Articles

Once a topic is claimed, the researcher runs `TOPIC_PROCESSING_ITERATIONS` iterations:

1. Parses task list checkboxes from the issue body
2. Separates incomplete (`- [ ]`) from completed (`- [x]`) articles
3. If incomplete articles exist:
   - Picks one randomly
   - Creates the article from web sources
   - Marks the checkbox as done (`- [x]`)
   - Runs `IMPROVEMENTS_PER_NEW_ARTICLE` improvement passes
4. If all articles are complete:
   - Picks a completed article randomly
   - Runs one improvement iteration (Mode A or Mode B)
5. Posts a summary comment with all changes made
6. Unassigns itself from the topic

